    "q-121589": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121589",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223941/assets/ad22dcd0793dbd4f7135598f94d3cfce514b8966bb18ea7edbe34058c208404e.png)\n\nA child is tearing the border of a paper party cone-shaped hat in such a way that the volume inside the hat decreases at $10\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the cone is always two times its base radius. At what rate is the radius of the hat decreasing when the radius is $15\\,\\text{in}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $h$ the height of the hat and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always two times its base diameter. So, we have $h=2r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(2r) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =2\\pi (15)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =450\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{45\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the hat is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{45\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
