      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223941/assets/b18b1e3a686f70a4c583324ddc253e8d334f63f017639bd47f6f483d42582801.png)\n\nAn inverted conical dispenser has a diameter of $100\\,\\text{in}$ and a depth of $110\\,\\text{in}.$ If a liquid is flowing out of the vertex of container at a rate of $9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}$, how fast is the height of the liquid dropping when the liquid is $60\\,\\text{in}$ deep?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=110\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the container, and $R=\\frac{100}{2}=50\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=60\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{110} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5h}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{5h}{11})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi (60)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{90\\,000}{121}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water height is decreasing at a rate of $12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$",
