    "q-359420": {
      "dom_id": "step-q359420",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223941/assets/ffdfbbfcbc2a01e79fd9490f05744b2d0bf1d24dd616a0b318ee95acfa26e9ba.png)\n\nA cone-shaped pile of salt is being depleted so that the volume of the pile decreases at $5\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{hr}.$ The height of the pile is always one-half of its base diameter. Find the rate of the pile’s height decrease when the height is $5\\,\\text{m}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe height is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $h$ the height of the pile and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-5\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{hr},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always one-half of its base diameter. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{2}d \\\\ h & =\\frac{1}{2}(2r) \\\\ r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (h)^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pi  & =\\pi (5)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -5\\pi  & =25\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the pile is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
